#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""rootctl - one switch for BOTH schedulers of the interim headless root.

    rootctl stop     -> Hub scheduling OFF and headless-root.timer OFF (both off)
    rootctl start    -> Hub scheduling ON  and headless-root.timer ON  (both on)
    rootctl status   -> readonly observation of both actual states

This is NOT a cutover switch: nothing here turns one scheduler off because the
other exists, and `stop` is never inverted. `start` is deliberately fail-closed:
Hub first, verified, then the timer, verified; any unknown, malformed,
mismatched, auth-denied, timed-out or unreachable readback exits non-zero with
the truthful partial state. A failed `start` restores the state its own pre-check
observed instead of disabling scheduling it did not enable. `stop` is
best-effort on both sides and never re-enables the Hub when the timer step fails.

In-flight root runs are never disturbed: rootctl never stops or kills
headless-root.service or its transient scopes, and never touches root.lock,
state.json or the runs/ directory.

Verdicts are derived from actual readbacks only, never assumed:
hub (on|off|unknown) x timer (on|off|unknown) ->
    both_on | both_off | hub_on_timer_off | hub_off_timer_on
    hub_on_timer_unknown | hub_off_timer_unknown
    hub_unknown_timer_on | hub_unknown_timer_off | hub_unknown_timer_unknown

Hub contract (owned by Hub head 6e966c):
    GET  /api/root/scheduling -> {"enabled": <strict bool>, "updated_at": <UTC>}
    PUT  /api/root/scheduling <- {"enabled": <strict bool>}
Only /api/root/scheduling is ever called; rootctl never reads offers, never
claims a start and never creates a run. While the Hub reports disabled it
guards its own offers/claims/start transitions.

Admin credential: rootctl never guesses which credential it holds. The config
must state `auth_mode` explicitly. A bearer `token` must be an ADMIN credential
of the Hub; the shared host-quota token in .local/quota-hub.json is a host
quota credential and is NOT an admin credential, so that file cannot be used
unqualified. Session auth uses the CSRF token and cookie already held by an
existing Hub custodian session (`csrf_token`, `cookie`), pasted into the config
by the custodian. rootctl creates no credential, reads no credential store and
never prints one.

There is no session pause: the Hub defines no pause endpoint, so `--pause-session`
does not exist and argparse refuses it. Any later pause needs a Hub contract
first and a new explicit human decision.

Configuration uses the existing supported formats only (scripts/quota-gate.py):
a JSON config with hub_url/token/auth_mode/host_id/timeout_sec (default
.local/quota-hub.json, overridable by ROOTCTL_HUB_CONFIG or QUOTA_HUB_CONFIG),
and the ROOTCTL_HUB_URL / ROOTCTL_HUB_TOKEN / ROOTCTL_HUB_HOST_ID /
ROOTCTL_HUB_TIMEOUT environment overrides. The host quota-launcher variables
QUOTA_HUB_URL / QUOTA_HUB_TOKEN / QUOTA_HUB_HOST_ID / QUOTA_HUB_TIMEOUT are
never read here, so a host quota credential can never silently override the
admin config. No credential value is ever printed; the Hub URL is printed as
scheme://host:port only, never with a path, query or fragment.

Test knobs (offline author stage): ROOTCTL_SYSTEMCTL, ROOTCTL_TIMER_UNIT,
ROOTCTL_LOCK, ROOTCTL_LOCK_WAIT, ROOTCTL_HTTP_TIMEOUT.
"""
